<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Notarisation requires the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime
         kills this app on its first transcription without the one exception
         below. Kept to exactly one: every entitlement here is a hole in the
         protection notarisation is supposed to certify.

         Measured, not guessed — the same recording transcribed through a
         bundle signed four ways:

           nothing              → SIGKILL right after the model loads
           allow-jit            → SIGKILL, same place
           allow-unsigned-…     → works
           both                 → works

         So MLX does not ask for JIT pages the polite way (MAP_JIT); it maps
         memory that is writable and executable outright, which is what this
         entitlement permits and allow-jit does not.

         Notably absent: disable-library-validation. The 166 .so and .dylib
         files in the bundled runtime come from PyPI, but bundle.sh signs each
         of them with the same certificate as the app, so library validation
         has nothing to object to. Turning it off would have been the obvious
         thing to do and would have weakened the app for no reason. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Without this the app cannot even ask for the microphone under the
         hardened runtime: the request goes nowhere, no dialog appears, and
         Cyclop never shows up in System Settings → Microphone at all — the
         list only holds apps that managed to ask. Removed once as "sandbox
         only" after checking that transcription still worked, which it did:
         the worker reads a wav file and never touches the microphone. The
         recording side is in the app, and that is what broke. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The calendar tab, for the same reason: under the hardened runtime the
         request is refused before it reaches the system, no dialog appears,
         and Cyclop never joins System Settings → Calendars — a list with no
         "+" button, so there is no way in by hand either.

         The rule this file learned twice, at the cost of a broken microphone
         and a broken calendar: every NS…UsageDescription in Info.plist needs
         its entitlement here, or the permission it describes cannot be asked
         for at all. There are four descriptions and they are covered by the
         three entitlements below — the two calendar keys share one. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- NSAppleEventsUsageDescription: the fallback path for Music and
         Spotify, used when the Now Playing helper stops working. It would
         fail the same silent way, and finding out during an Apple update is
         the worst moment to learn it. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
